nst the target epic's `dependencies` and vice versa; no dependency\nreferences a nonexistent epic. Implementation-type parallelism was enumerated\nexhaustively: the only konflux-onboarding epic (BRANCH-B-E010) has no blocking\nedge in either direction with any of the eight general-implementation epics\n(E004, E005, E006, E007, A-E008, A-E009, B-E011, B-E012); the Rule 25 coupling\nis correctly enforced as the \"build pipeline green\" AC on B-E011 rather than a\nDAG edge. No license-validation → repo-onboarding or license-validation →\nkonflux-onboarding edge exists (B-E009 is parallel with B-E008 and B-E010).\nAll seven branch files set both `branch` (matching filename) and `gated_by:\nRHAISTRAT-9005-E001`; no main-plan epic lists a branch epic in its\ndependencies — main-plan gating on the admission outcome correctly goes through\nE001 itself. Branch-internal edges (B-E008→B-E010, B-E009→B-E011,\nB-E11→B-E12) are rule-justified (rules 5, 9, 12/13). The E004 → E006 edge is a\ngenuine data dependency (Dashboard consumes the per-project quota model, not\nmerely a configuration reference). The shared Kueue config surface between E004\nand the branch admission epics is correctly treated as Rule 15 parallel\ncoordination, not a dependency. Critical path 2 (main plan) with branch-B chain\nlength 4 fits the M-with-new-component heuristic (4-5).\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has a single team; component/team tuples match the architecture\ncontext (Training Operator is owned by the Distributed Workloads team —\nA-E009/B-E012 correctly use component Training Operator with that team;\nDashboard/RHOAI Dashboard is separate in E006). No scope duplication between\nsiblings: A-E009's queue configuration is explicitly test-scoped and separate\nfrom A-E008's product configuration (justified to preserve Rule 14\nparallelism); E004's quota configuration vs branch admission configuration are\ndistinct requirements (HLR-2 vs HLR-1) over shared objects, flagged as a\ncoordination risk rather than duplicated work; B-E011 (queueing/scheduler side)\nvs B-E012 (operator side) split along the component boundary. No epic bundles\ncode implementation with content authoring. No epic is visibly oversized\nrelative to a sprint.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n\nAll three Investigations are genuine: each resolves a strategy open question\nwhose outcome changes downstream scope or existence (E001 selects the branch\nand rewrites E006/E007; E002 rewrites E005; E003 removes E007). All\nImplementations produce concrete artifacts. Every epic with `gated_by` set has\nboth `action` and `fallback_approach` populated; every `gated_by` target is in\nthe epic's direct `dependencies`; every Investigation dependency is covered by\ngating metadata. One minor: E007 has two Investigation dependencies (E001 and\nE003) but the single-valued `gated_by` records only the E003 `remove` gate; the\nsecondary E001 `rewrite` gate is documented only in `gate_failure_impact` prose.\nThis is a representational limitation of the field, handled as well as the\nschema allows (the existence gate — the one structural to the DAG — is the one\nrecorded), and does not cause incorrect execution.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n\nSignal-set usage is type-correct everywhere: E001-E003 carry\n`investigation_signals` (ai_signals null); all seven Implementation epics and\nall seven branch epics carry `ai_signals` (investigation_signals null). All 14\nai-signals files exist, their table values match the frontmatter exactly, and\nthe rationales are substantive and consistent with the strategy and\narchitecture context. Spot-checks confirmed the honesty of the scoring: E002's\n`source_accessibility: 0` / `human_judgment_required: -2` correctly reflect\nthat customer preemption needs are unresolved field signals; E005's\n`architecture_claims: 0` is accurate (the Distributed Workloads architecture\ncontext documents queueing/admission but not preemption); A-E008's\n`architecture_claims: 0` correctly notes RayJob's Kueue admission integration\nis not documented (only PyTorchJob admission is); B-E011's\n`pattern_precedent: -1` matches the architecture context's \"Volcano is not\nshipped or supported\". E006's `architecture_claims: 1` is corroborated\n(dashboard.md documents CR-reading; distributed-workloads.md documents Kueue\nmetrics to Prometheus).\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n\nEvery epic has testable ACs with explicit measurement approaches. Rule-mandated\nACs: the konflux chain's \"build pipeline green\" AC is present on the integration\nepic (B-E011: \"The Konflux build pipeline for the scheduler plugin is green for\nthe revision being integrated\") in addition to B-E010's own pipeline ACs. No\nepic replaces an existing feature, so rollback/feature-flag ACs are not\ntriggered; no docs-authoring epic exists, so its review AC is not triggered.\nNo epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — all cross-references use\ndescriptive names (e.g., \"the gang-admission semantics investigation\", \"the\nper-project GPU quota enforcement work\"), which survive Jira creation.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n\nAll strategy scope is covered: every HLR, all three open questions (each becomes\nan Investigation), both out-of-scope constraints respected (no GPU sharing, no\nmulti-cluster queueing appear anywhere), and the problem statement's pain\npoints (piecemeal occupation, timeout deaths, starvation, mis-topologized\nplacement) are traceable to specific ACs. Conditional branches cover both\nbounded outcomes of Open Question 1 with full implementation chains, including\ncomponent onboarding for the new scheduler plugin under Branch B. Cross-epic\nconsistency holds: both job types (PyTorchJob, RayJob) are accounted for in\neach branch (Branch A: A-E008 covers the RayJob/Kuberay admission path; Branch\nB: B-E011 carries KubeRay pod-group wiring with an explicit contingency).\nStrategy-level context is carried forward, not dropped —